   "html_text": "Langer canvasses thousands of ethological examples, from the lifting of a leg by a dog to pee, to the flinging of a branch watched by a gorilla, to the grooming of baboons (MEHF II 149f., 168f.). All animal acts are, Langer argues, instinctive, failing to achieve the vital activity of forms characteristic of the evolved human being, as she thinks her study of the ethology of insects, wolves, lemurs, migratory birds, monkeys, peacocks, turkeys, elephants, gorillas, grizzly bears, and other animal",
